WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:08.134
I woke up very late the next morning. Tired from my visit to the lost continent of Atlantis, I slept until eleven o'clock.

2
00:00:08.135 --> 00:00:12.634
I dressed quickly and hurried to the gallery to learn where the Nautilus was heading.

3
00:00:12.635 --> 00:00:20.793
The instruments showed that it was still journeying southward at a speed of twenty miles per hour and a depth of three hundred feet.

4
00:00:20.794 --> 00:00:25.134
I spent most of the day watching the fish through the windows in the gallery.

5
00:00:25.135 --> 00:00:29.197
Around four o'clock my view was blocked by a high wall.

6
00:00:29.198 --> 00:00:37.610
It must have been a continent, or at least an island—perhaps one of the Canary Islands or Cape Verde Islands off the African coast.

7
00:00:37.611 --> 00:00:42.190
At this point the panels closed, and I decided to take a nap.

8
00:00:42.191 --> 00:00:45.094
I did not wake up until the following morning.

9
00:00:45.095 --> 00:00:50.927
I checked the instruments in the gallery and found that the Nautilus was floating on the surface of the water.

10
00:00:50.928 --> 00:00:55.279
But it was odd that I didn't feel any waves rocking the submarine.

11
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:03.176
I heard footsteps on the platform, so I climbed as far as the hatch. To my surprise, when I looked up, I didn't see daylight.

12
00:01:03.177 --> 00:01:06.755
Instead we were surrounded by a deep darkness.

13
00:01:06.756 --> 00:01:15.414
"Where are we?" I said out loud, though I didn't expect anyone to answer me. "Have I mistaken the time? Is it still night?"

14
00:01:15.415 --> 00:01:19.676
I was surprised when a voice above me said, "Is that you, Professor?"

15
00:01:19.677 --> 00:01:24.414
"Ah, Captain," I replied. "Where are we?"

16
00:01:24.415 --> 00:01:26.144
"Underground."

17
00:01:26.145 --> 00:01:31.652
"Underground!" I exclaimed. "How can the Nautilus be floating underground?"

18
00:01:31.653 --> 00:01:35.223
"Wait a few minutes until our light is on, and you'll see."

19
00:01:35.224 --> 00:01:41.390
I climbed up to the platform and waited. It was so dark that I couldn't even see Captain Nemo.

20
00:01:41.391 --> 00:01:46.811
But looking straight up, I perceived a dim light coming from a circular hole.

21
00:01:46.812 --> 00:01:52.724
At that moment the submarine's bright light came on, and I took the opportunity to look around.

22
00:01:52.725 --> 00:01:57.970
The Nautilus was still, floating on a circular lake that was two miles wide.

23
00:01:57.971 --> 00:02:03.200
This lake was imprisoned by walls that were five hundred yards high and slanted inward.

24
00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:08.351
At the top of the walls was the circular opening through which I could now see daylight.

25
00:02:08.352 --> 00:02:12.105
"Where are we?" I asked the captain again.

26
00:02:12.106 --> 00:02:16.026
"In the heart of an extinct volcano," he replied.

27
00:02:16.027 --> 00:02:24.764
"An earthquake caused the sea to invade it, and we entered through a natural canal that runs about thirty feet below the surface of the ocean.

28
00:02:24.765 --> 00:02:29.946
Show me any other harbor on earth that can offer such a perfect place of refuge."

29
00:02:29.947 --> 00:02:37.605
"You're surely safe in here," I said, "but what use is this refuge to you? The Nautilus doesn't really need a port."

30
00:02:37.606 --> 00:02:41.589
"No, though I like to think of this as our home port.

31
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:50.835
What the Nautilus needs is electricity. We make electricity from sodium; we get sodium from coal; and we mine coal right here."

32
00:02:50.836 --> 00:02:53.827
"So will I get to see your men mining coal?"

33
00:02:53.828 --> 00:02:58.743
"No, we don't have time for that. I'm in a hurry to continue our voyage around the world.

34
00:02:58.744 --> 00:03:03.081
The men will be loading some sodium that we've stored here," said the captain.

35
00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:10.319
"But we'll only be here for one day, so if you want to explore the lake and the volcano's crater, you'll have to do it today."

36
00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:17.144
I went in search of Ned and Conseil and found them in their cabin. I asked them to follow me up to the platform.

37
00:03:17.145 --> 00:03:24.469
Conseil, who was quite used to strange things, wasn't the least bit surprised to learn he'd woken up in the heart of an extinct volcano!

38
00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:29.890
Meanwhile Ned immediately began thinking of ways to find an exit from the volcano.

39
00:03:29.891 --> 00:03:36.120
"We are once more on land," Conseil declared as we stepped onto a dock on the lake's shore.

40
00:03:36.121 --> 00:03:43.612
"I don't call this land," said Ned grumpily, "and besides, we're not on land but under it."

41
00:03:43.613 --> 00:03:47.199
We found a path that wound around the crater's walls.

42
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.882
Some shrubs and even some trees grew from cracks in the walls.

43
00:03:51.883 --> 00:03:58.549
I noticed many beautiful, fragrant flowers and a variety of birds circled in the air above us.

44
00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:04.517
We had arrived at the base of some sturdy trees when Ned exclaimed, "Look! A hive!"

45
00:04:04.518 --> 00:04:08.271
"And there are bees humming around it!" said Conseil.

46
00:04:08.272 --> 00:04:12.089
I approached the hive and found that they were correct.

47
00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:16.525
Naturally Ned wanted to gather some honey, and I couldn't object.

48
00:04:16.526 --> 00:04:21.430
He quickly made a small fire and smoked the bees out of their hive.

49
00:04:21.431 --> 00:04:28.922
"When I have mixed this honey into some breadfruit," said Ned in a more cheerful voice, "I'll be able to offer you a delicious cake."

50
00:04:28.923 --> 00:04:32.438
"It'll be as sweet as gingerbread!" said Conseil.

51
00:04:32.439 --> 00:04:39.263
We climbed as close to the volcano's summit as we could with Ned trying unsuccessfully to capture some birds.

52
00:04:39.264 --> 00:04:44.486
When we returned to the dock, Captain Nemo's men were just finishing loading the sodium.

53
00:04:44.487 --> 00:04:49.248
The Nautilus could have left right then, but the captain did not give the order.

54
00:04:49.249 --> 00:04:55.494
I suspected that he wanted to wait until night so that no one would see us emerging from the secret entrance.

55
00:04:55.495 --> 00:05:03.494
After that we continued on our journey and the Nautilus crossed the Sargasso Sea, which was filled with mats of seaweed.

56
00:05:03.495 --> 00:05:08.819
For the next nineteen days the submarine stayed in the middle of the Atlantic.

57
00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.898
There was no chance of escape.

58
00:05:10.899 --> 00:05:20.390
I hoped that once Captain Nemo had completed his trip around the world, he would let us leave if we swore never to tell a soul about him and his submarine.

59
00:05:20.391 --> 00:05:23.136
But a part of me knew that would never happen.

60
00:05:23.137 --> 00:05:29.860
During this time I rarely saw the captain, but I often found his books left open in the library.

61
00:05:29.861 --> 00:05:33.613
One day I even found that he'd been reading my book.

62
00:05:33.614 --> 00:05:37.859
He had made notes in the margins where he disagreed with my findings.

63
00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:43.208
By this point it was March, and we had traveled thirteen thousand leagues.

64
00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:48.438
We had now reached a place where the Atlantic Ocean was supposed to be exceptionally deep.

65
00:05:48.439 --> 00:05:54.780
"We're going to try to reach the sea bottom today," Captain Nemo announced one morning when he found me in the gallery.

66
00:05:54.781 --> 00:05:59.185
He gave the orders and the Nautilus quickly began to descend.

67
00:05:59.186 --> 00:06:04.597
I could feel the submarine's steel plates shuddering as the water pressure increased.

68
00:06:04.598 --> 00:06:10.510
We passed the depths at which most fish could live and yet we continued to descend.

69
00:06:10.511 --> 00:06:16.510
Finally we reached the sea bottom at a depth of almost ten miles below the surface.

70
00:06:16.511 --> 00:06:22.262
"Look at these magnificent black rocks, these uninhabited caves!" I exclaimed.

71
00:06:22.263 --> 00:06:28.502
"No living thing can survive here. Oh, how I wish I could always remember these fabulous sights!"

72
00:06:28.503 --> 00:06:33.915
"You can," said the captain, and he called for a crew member to take a photograph for me.

73
00:06:33.916 --> 00:06:41.978
"Now we must ascend," he continued. "I don't want to expose the Nautilus to this much pressure for too long. Hold on tight."

74
00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:46.074
I was about to ask why, when I was suddenly thrown to the floor.

75
00:06:46.075 --> 00:06:51.058
The captain had signaled for the submarine to rise and we were shooting toward the surface.

76
00:06:51.059 --> 00:06:57.479
In a mere four minutes, the Nautilus emerged from the water like a flying fish.

